    DOCKER VS VMs

        - Docker containers and Virtual Machines (VMs) are both used to run applications in isolated environments, but they work in very different ways. The main difference is that VMs virtualize the hardware, while Docker containers virtualize the operating system environment.

        - VIRTUAL MACHINES
            - A Virtual Machine is essentially a complete computer running inside another physical computer. A hypervisor allows multiple VMs to share the same physical server, while each VM has its own guest operating system, such as Ubuntu or Windows. Each VM is allocated its own CPU, memory, storage, and other resources from the host machine, Because every VM includes a complete operating system, VMs are relatively large and resource-intensive. They also take longer to start because the entire guest operating system needs to boot. For example, if you have five VMs running on one physical server, you could have five separate operating systems running at the same time.

        - DOCKER CONTAINERS
            -  Docker takes a different approach. Instead of creating an entire virtual computer, Docker runs applications inside containers. Containers share the host machine's OS kernel, while keeping the application's processes, files, libraries, and dependencies isolated from other containers.

            - Docker uses the Docker Engine to create and manage these containers. Because containers don't need to include an entire operating system, they are much more lightweight than VMs. This means you can run many containers on the same server while using considerably fewer CPU and memory resources.
            For example, you could have:
            
