        - FROM
            - The FROM instruction defines the base image that Docker will use to build your image. For example, FROM python:3.12 starts with an image that already contains Python 3.12. This means you don't need to install Python yourself. The base image provides the operating system, runtime, or software environment that your application needs.
        - COPY
            - The COPY instruction copies files from your local machine into the Docker image. For example, COPY app.py /app/app.py takes the app.py file from your computer and places it inside the image at /app/app.py. This allows your application code to become part of the Docker image.
        - RUN
            - The RUN instruction executes commands during the Docker image build process. For example, RUN pip install flask, it intsalls flask into the image. You can also use RUN to install packages and dependencies, create directories, configure software, or compile application code. Remember that RUN happens when the image is being built, not when the container starts.
        - CMD
            -The CMD instruction tells Docker which command to execute when the container starts. For example, CMD ["python", "/app/app.py"] tells Docker to start the Python application when a container is created from the image.


