        -   MANAGED NODES GROUPS 
            - With managed node groups, AWS manages the underlying EC2 worker nodes for you. EKS handles tasks such as provisioning, updating and maintaining the nodes, reducing the amount of infrastructure management you need to do. The nodes are a ppart of an auto scaling group, allowing the number of nodes to increace or decreace depending on your workload.You can use On-Demand or Spot EC2 instances, depending on your cost and availability requirements.

        - SELF-MANAGED NODES
            - With self managed nodes, you are responsible for creating and managing the EC2 instances yourself and registering them with your EKS cluster. This gives you more control over the underlying infrastructure. You can use EKS-optimized AMIs or create your own custom AMIs with specific software and configurations. 
            - The advantage is greater customization and control, but the downside is that you have more responsibility for maintenance, updates, scaling, and configuration.
        AWS FARGATE
            - AWS Fargate provides a serverless way to run Kubernetes workloads with EKS. Instead of running your Pods on EC2 worker nodes, AWS manages the underlying infrastructure for you. You simply define the resources your Pods require, such as CPU and memory, and Fargate runs them without you having to manage servers.
            - This is the simplest option from an infrastructure-management perspective because there are no EC2 worker nodes for you to maintain but isnt always the cheapest.

